      "text": "We start by multiplying both sides by $x$:\n\\[ \\left(x - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) x = 4x \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 1 = 4x \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 4x - 1 = 0 \\]\nNext, we solve for $x$:\n\\[ x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1)}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 4}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{20}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = 2 \\pm \\sqrt{5} \\]\nNow, let's find the quantity $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$. We know that the quantity $x^3 + \\frac{1}{x^3}$ is:\n\\[ x^3 + \\frac{1}{x^3} = x \\left( x^2 - 4x - 1 \\right) + \\left(x - \\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x^3 + \\frac{1}{x^3} = 4x^2 - 2x^4 + 4x^2 - x \\]\n\\[ x^3 + \\frac{1}{x^3} = -2x^4 + 8x^2 - x \\]\nWe must subtract the equation $x^2 - 1 = 4x$ from it to solve for the quantity $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$:\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 8x^2 - 5x - 1 - (4x) \\]\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 8x^2 - 9x - 1 \\]\nThis expression can be factored as:\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = (2x - 1)(4x^2 + 4x + 1) \\]\nSubstituting $x^2 = 4x + 1$ gives us:\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = (2x - 1)(4(4x + 1) + 4x + 1) \\]\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = (2x - 1)(16x + 4 + 4x + 1) \\]\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = (2x - 1)(20x + 5) \\]\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = (2x - 1)(20(x + \\frac{1}{4})) \\]\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 20(2x - 1)(x + \\frac{1}{4}) \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = \\boxed{20(2x - 1)(x + \\frac{1}{4})}$ can only be deduced after the initial quadratic $x^2-4x-1=0$ has already been solved.",
